 TypeScript is a statically-typed programming language that builds upon JavaScript. It was developed by Microsoft to bring optional types, classes, and modules to the JavaScript world. This is particularly useful for large codebases or projects as it allows the developer to catch errors at compile time rather than runtime, reducing bugs and improving code quality.

Now let's discuss Duck Typing:

Duck typing is an approach to object-oriented design and type checking that relies on the presence (or "duck") of required properties and methods in the target object, as opposed to demanding specific inheritance from a base class. The name comes from the quote, "When I see a bird that walks like a duck and swims like a duck, I call that bird a duck," by Jim Fowler.

In TypeScript with duck typing, you don't need explicit inheritance or interface implementation for objects to be compatible with each other. Instead, an object is considered compatible if it has the same structure (properties and methods) as another object. For example, consider two objects A and B:

```typescript
interface IDuck {
  walk(): void;
  swim(): void;
}

const objectA = {
  walk() {},
  swim() {}
};

const objectB = {
  jump() {} // missing 'swim' method
};
```

Although `objectB` does not implement the `IDuck` interface or extend any specific base class, it will still be considered compatible by many libraries or functions that use duck typing because it has the same structure as `IDuck`. However, some libraries may raise warnings or errors due to lack of formal type checking. To avoid such issues, you can use TypeScript interfaces or classes to enforce strict typing and inheritance when needed. Duck typing is useful for less rigid scenarios where you want to accept multiple types as long as they have a common structure. This makes your code more versatile and easier to extend, but also potentially less predictable and harder to maintain if not used carefully. ⊕

 To create a Discord bot using Discord.js v14 with a slash command called `ban`, follow the steps below:

1. First, make sure you have Node.js installed on your system. If not, download it from https://nodejs.org/ and install it.

2. Create a new directory for your project:
```
mkdir myBot
cd myBot
```

3. Initialize a new Node.js project by running `npm init -y`.

4. Install Discord.js as a dependency:
```
npm install discord.js
```

5. Create a new file named `index.js` in the `myBot` folder and write the following code to set up the client:

```javascript
const { Client, Intents } = require('discord.js');

const client = new Client({ intents: [Intents.FLAGS.Guilds, Intents.FLAGS.GuildMessages, Intents.FLAGS.Bans] });

// Replace 'YOUR_TOKEN_HERE' with the actual token of your bot
client.login('YOUR_TOKEN_HERE');
```

6. Now, let's create a slash command named `ban`. In the same `index.js` file, add the following code at the end:

```javascript
const { ApplicationCommandData, ApplicationCommandOptionType, SlashCommandBuilder } = require('discord.js');

const command = new SlashCommandBuilder()
  .setName('ban')
  .setDescription('Ban a member from the server')
  .addUserOption((option) =>
    option
      .setName('member')
      .setDescription('The user to be banned')
      .setRequired(true),
  );

// Add the handler for the command here
```

7. Implement the `ban` command handler. Modify the code above as follows:

```javascript
async function runCommand({ interaction }) {
  const targetMember = interaction.guild.members.cache.get(interaction.options.get('member'));

  if (!targetMember) return;

  try {
    await targetMember.ban();
    interaction.reply(`Successfully banned ${targetMember.tag}`);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
    interaction.reply('An error occurred while trying to ban the member.');
  }
}
```

8. Register the `runCommand` function as a listener for the slash command:

```javascript
client.on('interactionCreate', async (interaction) => {
  // Make sure it's our slash command
  if (!command.match(interaction)) return;

  try {
    await runCommand({ interaction });
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
  }
});
```

9. Finally, add the imported module for the command builder at the beginning:

```javascript
const { CommandInteraction } = require('discord.js');
```

Now your bot is ready with a single slash command called `ban`. To test it, invite the bot to your server, run the script with `node index.js`, and use the slash command in Discord by typing `/ban @user` replacing '@user' with the username or ID of a member in your server.

Please note that this example does not include any error handling or permissions checks beyond what Discord provides, so be sure to implement those according to your needs before deploying the bot to production.

 To convert Markdown-formatted text to HTML-formatted text in C++, you can use a library like `hicmark` or `discount`. Here's an example using the `hicmark` library:

First, make sure you have the library installed. For more information about installation, please consult the official documentation (https://github.com/jgm213/HicMark).

Now, let's create a function called `mdToHtml`:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include "hicmark.hpp"

std::string mdToHtml(const std::string &md) {
  hicmark::Environment env;
  hicmark::Pander pander(&env);

  if (!pander.parseString(md)) {
    throw std::runtime_error("Failed to parse Markdown.");
  }

  std::ostringstream oss;
  oss << pander;

  return oss.str();
}
```

The `mdToHtml` function accepts a `std::string` referencing Markdown-formatted text as its argument. It initializes an instance of the `hicmark::Environment`, which is used to store configuration settings for the parser. The `hicmark::Pander` object is responsible for parsing and converting the input to HTML format.

The function attempts to parse the provided Markdown-formatted text using `pander.parseString(md)`. If parsing fails, an exception is thrown. Otherwise, the parsed HTML is stored in an `std::ostringstream` and returned as a `std::string`.

To use this function, simply call it with your Markdown-formatted string as an argument:

```cpp
int main() {
  std::string markdown = R"(
# Hello World!
This is some **marked up** text.
)";

  try {
    std::string html = mdToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << '\n';
  } catch (const std::exception &e) {
    std::cerr << "Error: " << e.what() << '\n';
  }

  return 0;
}
```

In the example above, the `mdToHtml` function is called with a simple Markdown-formatted string. The resulting HTML is printed to the console. If there's an error during the conversion process (e.g., invalid Markdown syntax), an exception will be caught and an error message will be printed to the console instead.

Remember that using external libraries may require additional dependencies and build steps. Be sure to read the documentation thoroughly before implementing this solution in your project.

**Update**: Here's an alternative solution using the CPP-MD library (https://github.com/vshymanskyy/cpp-md):

```cpp
#include <string>
#include "cpp-md.h"

 std::string mdToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
 static cppMd::Filters::Config config {};
 auto renderer = cppMd::makeRendererFromConfig<html>(config);
 std::ostringstream oss;
 oss << markdown << '\n';
 return renderHTML(renderer, oss.str().data());
 }
```

This function also converts Markdown to HTML using the CPP-MD library and prints the result as a string.

Please note that due to the different APIs, you should consult the CPP-MD documentation carefully to ensure proper usage.

`html.hpp` from the C++ Markdown project is required: https://github.com/vshymanskyy/cpp-md/blob/master/docs/html.md

