 To create an automated table of contents in a LaTeX document, you can follow these steps:

1. First, make sure that all sections and subsections in your document have appropriate `\section` and `\subsection` commands. For instance:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

% ... other packages, settings etc.

\title{My Example Document}
\author{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

... text ...

\subsection{Background}

... text ...

\subsection{Goals and Objectives}

... text ...

\section{Theory}

... text ...

\subsection{Related Work}

... text ...

% ... add more sections and subsections as needed
\end{document}
```

2. Now, to generate the table of contents, add the following command right below the `\maketitle`:

```latex
\tableofcontents
```

This command tells LaTeX to create and display the table of contents based on the hierarchy of sections and subsections in your document.

3. Compile your LaTeX document (e.g., using pdflatex). When you open the generated PDF file, you should see the table of contents at the beginning of the document, with each section and subsection listed as clickable links that take you directly to their respective locations in the text. ❯

Keep in mind that if you add or modify sections and subsections later in your document, the table of contents will need to be updated accordingly. To do this, simply recompile your LaTeX document with pdflatex or another compiler.

4. If you want to customize the appearance of the table of contents (e.g., change the font size, color, or style), you can use various packages like `tocbibind`, `titlesec`, or `fancyhdr`. For example, to change the format of the TOC entries using `titlesec`, add the following lines before the `\documentclass` command:

```latex
\usepackage{titlesec}

% Define a new formatting command for the \section title
\titleformat{\section}[hang]{ \normalfont\bfseries}{}{0em}{}[\textcolor{red}]
```

This will make all section titles appear in bold red font. Remember to compile your document after making any changes involving formatting packages. ❯

